// draft
// drafted
type Factor = { items: Text[] }
// drafted
type Combo = { values: Text[] }
// drafted
type GrayError = { code: Text, display: Text }

// drafted
too_few_items_err: GrayError = {code: "ValueError", display: "each iterable must have two or more items"}

gray_product(iterables: Factor[], repeat: Int) -> Result<Combo[], GrayError>
	note "every combination of one item per iterable, repeated repeat times, ordered so that only one value changes from one tuple to the next"
	# The output is too_few_items_err exactly when repeat is at least 1 and some iterable has fewer
	# than two items.
	// reaching for: an iff with a quantifier on each side, "repeat is at least 1 and some iterable
	// has fewer than two items" if and only if "out is the Err side holding too_few_items_err";
	// Err(...) cannot be compared as a value, and a line can end in only one quantifier
	# In an Ok output, every tuple's length is the number of iterables times repeat, or 0 when
	# repeat is below 1.
	all Ok(res) in out: all t in res: len(t.values) == len(iterables) * max(repeat, 0)
	# In an Ok output, every value in a tuple is an item of the iterable its position belongs to,
	# with the positions running through the iterables in order and starting over for each repeat.
	// reaching for: a quantifier over the positions p of a tuple, and the iterable at index p
	// modulo the number of iterables, so that the value at p is in that iterable's items
	# In an Ok output, every combination that takes one item from each position's iterable appears
	# as some tuple.
	// reaching for: a quantifier over every combination that takes one item from each position's
	// iterable (the cartesian product, with the iterables repeated repeat times), each found among
	// the tuples' values
	# If no iterable repeats an item, no two tuples in an Ok output are equal.
	all Ok(res) in out: unique res.values or (any f in iterables: not unique f.items)
	# In an Ok output, the first tuple takes the first item of every position's iterable.
	// reaching for: the list of each iterable's first item, written out repeat times in sequence
	// (list repetition), equal to the values of the tuple at position 0
	# If no iterable repeats an item, each tuple in an Ok output differs from the tuple before it in
	# exactly one position.
	// reaching for: a quantifier over pairs of consecutive tuples, and a count of the positions at
	// which their values differ, required to be exactly 1
	# In an Ok output, the value that changes from one tuple to the next moves to an item next to it
	# in its iterable.
	// reaching for: for consecutive tuples, the position where they differ and that position's
	// iterable, with the old and new values at adjacent indexes of the iterable's items
	# The output for a repeat of at least 1 is the output for the iterables written out that many
	# times in sequence with a repeat of 1.
	// reaching for: list repetition, the iterables written out repeat times in sequence, so the
	// line reads: when repeat is at least 1, out equals gray_product(<iterables repeated repeat
	// times>, 1)
	// example wanted: iterables "AB" and "CD" at repeat 1, the docstring's case: the order is
	// decided by which position changes first and where it turns back
	// example wanted: iterables "ABC" and "DE" at repeat 1: a three-item iterable shows the first
	// position running forward, then running back after the second position changes
	// example wanted: iterable "AB" at repeat 3, the docstring's equivalence with "AB", "AB", "AB"
	// example wanted: no iterables at repeat 1: the only combination is the empty one
	// example wanted: iterable "A" at repeat 0: the one-item iterable is never used, so whether the
	// error is raised is the case
	// example wanted: iterables "AB" and "C" at repeat 1: one iterable has a single item
	// example wanted: iterables "AA" and "CD" at repeat 1: repeated items, so tuples may be equal
	// by value and consecutive tuples may not differ
